 71	/**
 72	 * A step in a pipeline of an asynchronous computation. When the last step in the computation is
 73	 * complete, some objects captured during the computation are closed.
 74	 *
 75	 * <p>A pipeline of {@code ClosingFuture}s is a tree of steps. Each step represents either an
 76	 * asynchronously-computed intermediate value, or else an exception that indicates the failure or
 77	 * cancellation of the operation so far. The only way to extract the value or exception from a step
 78	 * is by declaring that step to be the last step of the pipeline. Nevertheless, we refer to the
 79	 * "value" of a successful step or the "result" (value or exception) of any step.
 80	 *
 81	 * <ol>
 82	 *   <li>A pipeline starts at its leaf step (or steps), which is created from either a callable
 83	 *       block or a {@link ListenableFuture}.
 84	 *   <li>Each other step is derived from one or more input steps. At each step, zero or more objects
 85	 *       can be captured for later closing.
 86	 *   <li>There is one last step (the root of the tree), from which you can extract the final result
 87	 *       of the computation. After that result is available (or the computation fails), all objects
 88	 *       captured by any of the steps in the pipeline are closed.
 89	 * </ol>
 90	 *
 91	 * <h3>Starting a pipeline</h3>
 92	 *
 93	 * Start a {@code ClosingFuture} pipeline {@linkplain #submit(ClosingCallable, Executor) from a
 94	 * callable block} that may capture objects for later closing. To start a pipeline from a {@link
 95	 * ListenableFuture} that doesn't create resources that should be closed later, you can use {@link
 96	 * #from(ListenableFuture)} instead.
 97	 *
 98	 * <h3>Derived steps</h3>
 99	 *
100	 * A {@code ClosingFuture} step can be derived from one or more input {@code ClosingFuture} steps in
101	 * ways similar to {@link FluentFuture}s:
102	 *
103	 * <ul>
104	 *   <li>by transforming the value from a successful input step,
105	 *   <li>by catching the exception from a failed input step, or
106	 *   <li>by combining the results of several input steps.
107	 * </ul>
108	 *
109	 * Each derivation can capture the next value or any intermediate objects for later closing.
110	 *
111	 * <p>A step can be the input to at most one derived step. Once you transform its value, catch its
112	 * exception, or combine it with others, you cannot do anything else with it, including declare it
113	 * to be the last step of the pipeline.
114	 *
115	 * <h4>Transforming</h4>
116	 *
117	 * To derive the next step by asynchronously applying a function to an input step's value, call
118	 * {@link #transform(ClosingFunction, Executor)} or {@link #transformAsync(AsyncClosingFunction,
119	 * Executor)} on the input step.
120	 *
121	 * <h4>Catching</h4>
122	 *
123	 * To derive the next step from a failed input step, call {@link #catching(Class, ClosingFunction,
124	 * Executor)} or {@link #catchingAsync(Class, AsyncClosingFunction, Executor)} on the input step.
125	 *
126	 * <h4>Combining</h4>
127	 *
128	 * To derive a {@code ClosingFuture} from two or more input steps, pass the input steps to {@link
129	 * #whenAllComplete(Iterable)} or {@link #whenAllSucceed(Iterable)} or its overloads.
130	 *
131	 * <h3>Cancelling</h3>
132	 *
133	 * Any step in a pipeline can be {@linkplain #cancel(boolean) cancelled}, even after another step
134	 * has been derived, with the same semantics as cancelling a {@link Future}. In addition, a
135	 * successfully cancelled step will immediately start closing all objects captured for later closing
136	 * by it and by its input steps.
137	 *
138	 * <h3>Ending a pipeline</h3>
139	 *
140	 * Each {@code ClosingFuture} pipeline must be ended. To end a pipeline, decide whether you want to
141	 * close the captured objects automatically or manually.
142	 *
143	 * <h4>Automatically closing</h4>
144	 *
145	 * You can extract a {@link Future} that represents the result of the last step in the pipeline by
146	 * calling {@link #finishToFuture()}. All objects the pipeline has captured for closing will begin
147	 * to be closed asynchronously <b>after</b> the returned {@code Future} is done: the future
148	 * completes before closing starts, rather than once it has finished.
149	 *
150	 * {@snippet :
151	 * FluentFuture<UserName> userName =
152	 *     ClosingFuture.submit(
153	 *             closer -> closer.eventuallyClose(database.newTransaction(), closingExecutor),
154	 *             executor)
155	 *         .transformAsync((closer, transaction) -> transaction.queryClosingFuture("..."), executor)
156	 *         .transform((closer, result) -> result.get("userName"), directExecutor())
157	 *         .catching(DBException.class, e -> "no user", directExecutor())
158	 *         .finishToFuture();
159	 * }
160	 *
161	 * In this example, when the {@code userName} {@link Future} is done, the transaction and the query
162	 * result cursor will both be closed, even if the operation is cancelled or fails.
163	 *
164	 * <h4>Manually closing</h4>
165	 *
166	 * If you want to close the captured objects manually, after you've used the final result, call
167	 * {@link #finishToValueAndCloser(ValueAndCloserConsumer, Executor)} to get an object that holds the
168	 * final result. You then call {@link ValueAndCloser#closeAsync()} to close the captured objects.
169	 *
170	 * {@snippet :
171	 *     ClosingFuture.submit(
172	 *             closer -> closer.eventuallyClose(database.newTransaction(), closingExecutor),
173	 *             executor)
174	 *     .transformAsync((closer, transaction) -> transaction.queryClosingFuture("..."), executor)
175	 *     .transform((closer, result) -> result.get("userName"), directExecutor())
176	 *     .catching(DBException.class, e -> "no user", directExecutor())
177	 *     .finishToValueAndCloser(
178	 *         valueAndCloser -> this.userNameValueAndCloser = valueAndCloser, executor);
179	 *
180	 * // later
181	 * try { // get() will throw if the operation failed or was cancelled.
182	 *   UserName userName = userNameValueAndCloser.get();
183	 *   // do something with userName
184	 * } finally {
185	 *   userNameValueAndCloser.closeAsync();
186	 * }
187	 * }
188	 *
189	 * In this example, when {@code userNameValueAndCloser.closeAsync()} is called, the transaction and
190	 * the query result cursor will both be closed, even if the operation is cancelled or fails.
191	 *
192	 * <p>Note that if you don't call {@code closeAsync()}, the captured objects will not be closed. The
193	 * automatic-closing approach described above is safer.
194	 *
195	 * @param <V> the type of the value of this step
196	 * @since 30.0
197	 */
